[Event "Scotch Gambit: the gambit itself"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/89PLb4a2/9yI8g1Um"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis"]

{ Today we learn the Scotch Gambit. Give a pawn, get every piece out fast. [%cal Ye2e4] }
1. e4 { The king pawn steps up and opens two roads at once. } (1. d4 { A great move, and a whole different world. } 1... Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 d5 { The queen pawn game. Today we want the open one. }) 1... e5 { He meets you head on. Bring the king knight out and hit that pawn. [%cal Yg1f3] } 2. Nf3 { The knight attacks his e pawn, so he has to answer you. [%cal Gf3e5] } (2. Bc4 { A fine move, and the bishop loves that square. But the knight comes first. } 2... Nf6 3. Nc3 Nc6 4. d3 Bb4 { Sound and quiet. We want the pawn break. }) (2. d4 { The centre game. Playable, but your queen comes out far too early. } 2... exd4 3. Nf3 Bb4+ 4. Bd2 Bxd2+ { Fine, and much less fun than our line. }) (2. Nc3 { Also good. But this knight has no target yet. } 2... Nf6 3. Nf3 Nc6 4. Bb5 Nd4 { A four knights game. Sound, and a different study. }) 2... Nc6 { He defends with the knight. Now break the centre open: pawn to d4. [%cal Yd2d4] } 3. d4 { Right in the middle. You offer a pawn to blast the position open. [%csl Gd4] } (3. Bb5 { The Spanish, and the engine likes it best. But it is a huge book. } 3... a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O b5 { Excellent chess. Just not today. }) (3. Bc4 { The Italian. Perfectly good, and a lot slower. } 3... Bc5 4. d3 Nf6 5. c3 d6 { A long quiet game. We want it open. }) (3. Nc3 { Fine, but it lets him plant a knight on d4. } 3... Nf6 4. Bb5 Nd4 5. Nxd4 exd4 { Playable, and your pawn break has gone. }) 3... exd4 { He grabs it. Now the gambit: bishop to c4, staring at f7. [%cal Yf1c4] } 4. Bc4 { The Scotch Gambit. Forget the pawn. Bishop and knight are already out. [%csl Gf7] } (4. Nxd4 { The plain Scotch, and the engine likes it a shade more. } 4... Nf6 5. Nxc6 bxc6 6. e5 Qe7 { Sound and good. But there is no gambit and no fun. }) (4. Qxd4 { Look again: which black piece is staring straight at d4? } 4... Nxd4 5. Nxd4 d5 { Your queen went for a knight and a pawn. }) (4. c3 { The Danish idea. Playable, but you hand over a second pawn. } 4... dxc3 5. Nxc3 Bc5 6. Bc4 d6 { Fun, but two pawns is a lot to owe. }) 1-0


[Event "Scotch Gambit: the main line with e5"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/89PLb4a2/Ss79RpcV"]
[FEN "r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/8/2BpP3/5N2/PPP2PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 1 4"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/8/2BpP3/5N2/PPP2PPP/RNBQK2R_b_KQkq_-_1_4"]

{ Out comes his king knight, hitting your e pawn. What do you do about it? }
4... Nf6 5. e5 { Push it. The pawn kicks his knight and grabs a chunk of space. [%csl Ge5] } (5. O-O { Also main theory and perfectly good. You castle and carry on. } 5... Nxe4 6. Re1 d5 7. Bxd5 Qxd5 8. Nc3 Qd8 { Sharp and playable. But we push the pawn. }) (5. Nxd4 { Taking the pawn back looks safe. But he is quicker than you here. } 5... Nxe4 6. Bxf7+ Kxf7 7. Qh5+ g6 8. Qd5+ Kg7 { Sharp and unclear, and the engine says he is a shade better. }) (5. Ng5 { The knight lunges at f7. He just shuts the door with the d pawn. } 5... d5 6. exd5 Qe7+ 7. Kf1 Ne5 { Your king cannot castle and you are worse. }) 5... d5 { He blocks with the d pawn and hits your bishop. Where does it go? } 6. Bb5 { Lovely. The bishop steps aside and pins his knight to the king. [%cal Gb5c6,Gb5e8] } (6. Bd3 { A safe square, but the bishop does nothing at all there. } 6... Ne4 7. Nbd2 Nxd2 8. Bxd2 Bg4 { Even, and your pieces have no bite. }) (6. Bb3 { The bishop hides, and his knight jumps to e4 for free. } 6... Ne4 7. O-O Bg4 8. c3 Bc5 { He is better and your bishop is boxed in. }) (6. exd6 { You can take in passing. But it opens everything up for him. } 6... Bxd6 7. Qe2+ Be7 8. Bg5 O-O { His pieces fly out and you are worse. }) 6... Ne4 { His knight hops to e4. Do not panic about it. What can you win back? } 7. Nxd4 { Yes. The pawn comes home and your knight lands in the middle. [%csl Gd4] } (7. O-O { Castling is good and safe. But the pawn on d4 is still his. } 7... Bg4 8. c3 dxc3 9. Nxc3 Nxc3 { Playable, but you never did get the pawn back. }) (7. c3 { The pawn tries to take back. He simply grabs and swaps off. } 7... dxc3 8. Nxc3 Bb4 9. O-O Bxc3 { Your centre is gone and he is fine. }) (7. Nbd2 { Chasing his knight, but it only trades pieces off. } 7... Bc5 8. Qe2 Nxd2 9. Qxd2 O-O { Level, and your pin has melted away. }) 7... Bd7 { The bishop comes to d7 to hold the knight. Do you have a trade here? } 8. Bxc6 { Nice. You take, and his pawns end up doubled and ugly. [%csl Rc6,Rc7] } (8. O-O { Safe, but the moment for the trade has gone. } 8... Nxd4 9. Bxd7+ Qxd7 10. Qxd4 Bc5 { Level, and his pieces are happy. }) (8. Nxc6 { The wrong piece takes, and your bishop on b5 has no job left. } 8... bxc6 9. Bd3 Qe7 10. Qe2 Qxe5 { He wins the pawn back and stands better. }) (8. Nb3 { The knight retreats for no reason and lets his queen out. } 8... Qh4 9. O-O O-O-O 10. Bxc6 Bxc6 { All his pieces are out and yours are not. }) 8... bxc6 { The b pawn takes back. Your king has been standing around a while. } 9. O-O { Well played. King safe, rook joins in, and his pawns are a mess. [%csl Rc6,Rc7] } (9. Nc3 { Development, but your king is still sitting in the middle. } 9... Qe7 10. Bf4 Qh4 11. Be3 Bb4 { He gets going first. Castle before this. }) (9. Nf3 { The knight steps back and gives up the strong square. } 9... Bc5 10. O-O O-O 11. Qd3 Be7 { Equal, and you moved that knight twice. }) (9. Bf4 { Developing, but his queen arrives with a hit. } 9... Qh4 10. Be3 Qe7 11. f3 Nd6 { Fine for him. The king comes first. }) 1-0


[Event "Scotch Gambit: the knight jumps to e4"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/89PLb4a2/KAuAKn3c"]
[FEN "r1bqkb1r/pppp1ppp/2n2n2/4P3/2Bp4/5N2/PPP2PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 0 5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkb1r/pppp1ppp/2n2n2/4P3/2Bp4/5N2/PPP2PPP/RNBQK2R_b_KQkq_-_0_5"]

{ This time the knight jumps to e4 instead of blocking. How do you meet it? }
5... Ne4 6. Qe2 { Good. The queen looks straight down the file and the knight must run. [%cal Ge2e4] } (6. O-O { Also excellent, and the engine even likes it a shade more. } 6... Be7 7. Re1 d5 8. exd6 Nxd6 9. Bd5 { A fine game too. But we like the queen move. }) (6. Bd5 { The bishop chases the knight. Playable, but it drifts. } 6... Nc5 7. O-O Be7 8. c3 dxc3 { Equal, and your bishop is far from home. }) (6. c3 { Grabbing the pawn back too early. His break comes first. } 6... d5 7. exd6 Nxd6 8. Bd3 Qe7+ { His queen checks and you are worse. }) 6... Nc5 { The knight hops to c5, eyeing your bishop. What have you not done yet? } 7. O-O { Yes. King tucked away, rook ready, and that pawn on d4 can wait. } (7. c3 { Also good, and the engine likes it just as much. } 7... d3 8. Qe3 Be7 9. b4 Ne6 { Playable. But castling first is easier to play. }) (7. Ng5 { The knight lunges at f7 again. He covers it and you gained nothing. } 7... Ne6 8. Nxe6 dxe6 9. O-O Bd7 { Equal, and you traded your best knight. }) (7. Bd5 { Chasing again, but the bishop is doing his work for him. } 7... b6 8. c4 dxc3 9. Nxc3 Ba6 { Your good bishop gets traded off. }) 7... Ne6 { His knight settles on e6. That pawn on d4 is still not yours. } 8. c3 { Good. The little pawn will take on d4 and rebuild your centre. [%cal Gc3d4] } (8. Bxe6 { Just as good by the numbers. But it hands him the open f file. } 8... fxe6 9. Rd1 Be7 10. Nxd4 Nxd4 { Equal, and his rook now has a road. }) (8. Rd1 { The rook lines up on the pawn. But it is slower than the pawn move. } 8... d5 9. exd6 Bxd6 10. c3 O-O { Fine, just a shade worse. }) (8. Re1 { The rook picks the wrong file. Nothing is happening there. } 8... Be7 9. c3 O-O 10. Qe4 d5 { He is comfortable and you are drifting. }) 8... d5 { He runs his d pawn two squares past yours. Look very carefully here. } 9. exd6 { In passing! Your pawn takes as if his had only stepped one square. [%csl Gd6] } (9. Bb3 { The bishop steps back, and now his big centre is real. } 9... Bc5 10. Rd1 a5 11. a4 O-O { He is comfortable and your e pawn is stuck. }) (9. Rd1 { Look again: what is happening to your bishop on c4? } 9... dxc4 10. Qxc4 Ne7 11. Na3 Qd5 { A bishop gone for one pawn. }) (9. Bb5 { The bishop runs sideways. Safe, but the moment has gone. } 9... dxc3 10. Nxc3 a6 11. Ba4 b5 { He kicks you around and keeps the centre. }) 1-0


[Event "Scotch Gambit: he holds the pawn with the bishop"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/89PLb4a2/BlDrqTWO"]
[FEN "r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/8/2BpP3/5N2/PPP2PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 1 4"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/8/2BpP3/5N2/PPP2PPP/RNBQK2R_b_KQkq_-_1_4"]

{ His bishop swings out to c5 and holds the extra pawn. Your move. }
4... Bc5 5. c3 { Nice. The little pawn offers a trade and builds a wall behind it. [%cal Gc3d4] } (5. Ng5 { The knight lunges at f7. It is playable, and very messy. } 5... Nh6 6. Bxf7+ Nxf7 7. Nxf7 Kxf7 { The queen check on h5 wins the bishop back. Level, and messy. }) (5. Bxf7+ { Look again: what does his king do after he takes? } 5... Kxf7 6. c3 d5 7. cxd4 Bb4+ { A bishop given away, and his king is perfectly safe. }) (5. O-O { Castling is sensible. But he simply keeps the extra pawn. } 5... d6 6. c3 Bg4 7. Qb3 Bxf3 { Playable, and a lot less clear. }) 5... Nf6 { He develops the knight and eyes your e pawn. What about that pawn on d4? } 6. cxd4 { Exactly. You take back and now two pawns stand side by side. [%csl Gd4,Ge4] } (6. e5 { Also good, and worth knowing. The pawn kicks his knight. } 6... d5 7. Bb5 Ne4 8. cxd4 Bb6 { Just as good. But we want the two pawn centre. }) (6. O-O { Castling first lets him grab on e4. } 6... Nxe4 7. cxd4 d5 8. dxc5 dxc4 { Messy, and he holds the extra pawn. }) (6. b4 { The pawn hits the bishop, but you forget the middle. } 6... Bb6 7. a4 a6 8. O-O Nxe4 { He takes your pawn and stays better. }) 6... Bb4+ { His bishop checks from b4. Several men can block. Which one? } 7. Bd2 { The right blocker. It offers a trade and keeps everything solid. } (7. Nbd2 { Also completely fine. The knight blocks instead. } 7... Nxe4 8. O-O Nxd2 9. Bxd2 Bxd2 { Equal, and a lot of pieces come off. }) (7. Nc3 { The old gambit line. You give the e pawn away for speed. } 7... Nxe4 8. O-O Bxc3 9. d5 Bf6 { Wild and playable. But we keep it simple. }) (7. Kf1 { Blocking with nothing at all. Your king moves and the rook sleeps. } 7... d5 8. exd5 Nxd5 9. Nc3 Bxc3 { You are worse for no reason. }) 7... Bxd2+ { He takes on d2. Two of your pieces can take back. Choose the best. } 8. Nbxd2 { Perfect. The queen knight takes and every piece is ready to go. [%csl Gd2] } (8. Qxd2 { The queen takes, but she sits loose in front of your king. } 8... Nxe4 9. Qe2 Qe7 10. O-O Nf6 { He wins the pawn back with ease. }) (8. Nfxd2 { The wrong knight. Now look at the pawn on d4. } 8... Nxd4 9. O-O d6 10. Nb3 Nxb3 { A pawn gone and your centre with it. }) (8. Kxd2 { Never with the king. Look at what comes with check. } 8... Nxe4+ 9. Ke1 d5 10. Be2 O-O { A pawn down and your rooks will never meet. }) 1-0


[Event "Scotch Gambit: the bishop check on b4"]
