{ He checks from b4 straight away. How do you deal with that? }
4... Bb4+ 5. c3 { Good. Block with the pawn and offer him a trade in the middle. [%cal Gc3d4] } (5. Bd2 { Blocking with the bishop is fine too, and very calm. } 5... Bxd2+ 6. Qxd2 Nf6 7. Bd5 O-O { Even. But the pawn move fights for more. }) (5. Nbd2 { The knight blocks. Playable, but it clogs up your own pieces. } 5... Nf6 6. O-O O-O 7. e5 Ng4 { He is perfectly comfortable. }) (5. Kf1 { Moving the king throws your castling away. } 5... Nf6 6. a3 Bc5 7. b4 Be7 { You are worse for no reason at all. }) 5... dxc3 { He takes the pawn. Two of your men can take it back. Which one? } 6. bxc3 { The b pawn. Your centre grows and the rook gets the open b file. [%cal Ga1b1] } (6. Nxc3 { Also perfectly good. The knight develops as it captures. } 6... d6 7. Ng5 Nh6 8. O-O O-O { Even and pleasant. But the pawn keeps more pawns. }) (6. O-O { You can leave it and let him keep the pawn. } 6... Nf6 7. bxc3 Bc5 8. e5 d5 { Playable, but the plain recapture is cleaner. }) (6. Kf1 { Never move the king here. He simply grabs another pawn. } 6... cxb2 7. Bxb2 Nf6 8. e5 Nh5 { Two pawns down and your rook is stuck. }) 6... Ba5 { The bishop slides back to a5. Your king is still in the middle. } 7. O-O { Yes. Get castled. Now every piece you own is doing something. } (7. Qb3 { The queen doubles on f7. Tempting, but he defends easily. } 7... Qf6 8. O-O Nge7 9. Bg5 Qg6 { Playable, but the queen came out early. }) (7. Ba3 { Stopping him from castling looks clever. But the bishop is stranded. } 7... Nge7 8. Ng5 d5 9. exd5 Ne5 { He unwinds and you are worse. }) (7. Nbd2 { The knight picks a passive square. } 7... Nge7 8. O-O h6 9. Bb3 O-O { Fine for him, and your knight has no future. }) 1-0


[Event "Scotch Gambit: the quiet d6"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/89PLb4a2/WfUCmwvV"]
[FEN "r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/8/2BpP3/5N2/PPP2PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 1 4"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/8/2BpP3/5N2/PPP2PPP/RNBQK2R_b_KQkq_-_1_4"]

{ He plays the quiet d6 and simply hangs on to the pawn. How do you answer? }
4... d6 5. Nxd4 { Simple and strong. No gambit needed here. Take the pawn straight back. [%csl Gd4] } (5. O-O { Also good, and just as playable. } 5... Bg4 6. h3 Bxf3 7. Qxf3 Qd7 8. Qb3 { A pleasant game. But taking the pawn is simpler. }) (5. Bb5 { The pin is playable, but it trades off your best bishop. } 5... Bd7 6. O-O Be7 7. Bxc6 Bxc6 { Equal, and the fun has gone. }) (5. Ng5 { The lunge at f7 does not work here. He guards it easily. } 5... Nh6 6. Nf3 Qf6 7. Bb5 Qg6 { Your knight went back and forth for nothing. }) 5... Nf6 { His knight comes out and eyes your e pawn. Your king is still in the middle. } 6. O-O { Well done. King safe first, and the e pawn is fine where it is. } (6. Nc3 { Also good, and it guards the e pawn as well. } 6... Be7 7. O-O O-O 8. a4 Re8 { Just as pleasant. Both moves are fine. }) (6. Nxc6 { Trading helps him: his b pawn comes to the middle. } 6... bxc6 7. Nc3 Be7 8. b3 a5 { Still fine for you, but you gave away the tension. }) (6. Bg5 { The pin does nothing here, and he breaks it at once. } 6... h6 7. Bxf6 Qxf6 8. Nb5 Qd8 { You traded a good bishop for a knight. }) 6... Be7 { He tucks the bishop in and will castle next. One of your pieces is missing. } 7. Nc3 { Perfect. The last knight comes out and your whole army is awake. [%csl Gc3] } (7. Nxc6 { Trading again. His b pawn marches to the middle and helps him. } 7... bxc6 8. Nc3 a5 9. b3 O-O { Still fine, but you helped his pawns along. }) (7. Re1 { The rook is useful, but the knight comes first. } 7... Nxd4 8. Qxd4 O-O 9. Nc3 Ng4 { Good for you. Just a shade slower. }) (7. Bb5 { The pin gives away your best bishop again. } 7... Bd7 8. Nxc6 Bxc6 9. Bxc6+ bxc6 { Equal, and the attacking bishop is gone. }) 1-0


[Event "Scotch Gambit: he takes with the knight"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
