{ He takes with the knight instead of the pawn. Do not just grab it back. }
3... Nxd4 4. Nxd4 { Right. Knights come off first. Only then is d4 safe. [%csl Gd4] } (4. Qxd4 { Look again: what is his e pawn attacking? } 4... exd4 { The pawn eats your queen. Trade the knights off first. }) (4. Nxe5 { You can take that one too. Playable, but the knight gets chased. } 4... Ne6 5. Nc4 b5 6. Ne3 Bb7 { Sound, and much harder to handle. }) (4. Bc4 { The gambit bishop, but the moment for it has passed. } 4... Nxf3+ 5. Qxf3 Qe7 6. Nc3 c6 { He has traded a piece and is doing fine. }) 4... exd4 { He takes back with the pawn. Now d4 is just a pawn standing alone. } 5. Qxd4 { Yes. Now the queen may take, and she stands in the middle. [%csl Gd4] } (5. Bc4 { Also playable. You leave the pawn and develop instead. } 5... d5 6. Bxd5 Nf6 7. Qxd4 Be7 { A good game too. But the pawn was free. }) (5. Be2 { Quiet, and it lets him hold the extra pawn. } 5... Bc5 6. O-O a5 7. c3 dxc3 { He is a pawn up and comfortable. }) (5. c3 { Offering another pawn is not needed here. } 5... d5 6. Qxd4 dxe4 7. Qxe4+ Be7 { Level, and you gave the centre away. }) 5... Qf6 { His queen offers a trade and hits your queen. Where does she go? } 6. Qc4 { Good. She sidesteps and joins the bishop diagonal aimed at f7. [%csl Gf7] } (6. e5 { Kicking the queen is tempting. But the trade helps him. } 6... Qb6 7. Qxb6 axb6 8. Nc3 c6 { Equal, and his rook gets the open file. }) (6. Qe3 { Also fine, though she blocks your own bishop. } 6... Bb4+ 7. c3 Be7 8. Bd3 Qh6 { Pleasant for you, just a little clumsy. }) (6. Qc3 { Offering the trade is safe but dull. } 6... Qxc3+ 7. Nxc3 Bb4 8. Bd2 d6 { Even, and all the fun has gone. }) 1-0
